  "text" : "BF16 makes training faster because each number is half the bits of FP32.\nFewer bits means more numbers per memory read, and more multiply adds per tensor core cycle.\nA B200 peaks near 2250 teraflops in BF16, FP8 doubles that.\nFP16 is also 16 bits, but it spends them on precision.\nSo anything past 65504 will overflow.\nBF16 keeps FP32's 8 exponent bits.\nSame range, less precision, and training stays stable.\nFP8 keeps just 3 bits, so training in it needs a scaling library like NVIDIA's Transformer\nEngine.\nIn PyTorch, wrap your forward pass in AutoCast with BF16, you get faster math without the\noverflow."
